.TH DRAW3DSAFETEST 1
.SH NAME
draw3dsafetest \- check that no draw3d argument can fault emu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B draw3dsafetest
[
.I provider
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Limbo program must not be able to bring down the virtual machine it
runs on, whatever arguments it passes.
.I Draw3dsafetest
is about that property and no other: it checks not one pixel, and a case
here passing says nothing whatever about what was drawn.
It says the VM was still standing afterwards.
.PP
The reason
.IR draw3d (2)
is worth singling out is that one of its two providers is not Limbo.
.B /dis/math/draw3ddev.dis
encodes each call as a message and writes it to
.IR draw (3),
where it is parsed by C in
.B emu/port/devdraw.c
that indexes pixel memory directly; an index that goes out of range
there reads whatever address the arithmetic produced, rather than
raising the bounds error the Limbo provider would.
That is why
.I provider
defaults to
.B /dis/math/draw3ddev.dis
rather than to the software
.BR /dis/math/draw3d.dis .
Running it against the software provider as well is still worth doing \-
a fault there would be a bug in the VM itself \- and is what the
argument is for.
.PP
The arguments are chosen for their arithmetic and not for their meaning:
widths and heights of 0,
.BR \-1 ,
and up to
.BR 16r7FFFFFFF ,
which travel to the
.B J
message as a raw 32\-bit integer with no upper bound;
angles of
.if n 1e30
.if t 10\u\s-230\s+2\d
and its negative;
centres far outside the destination;
masks that are not the same size as the source, which is the case where
the two index computations in the C sprite loop disagree;
line thicknesses up to the same limit;
polygons at 1023, 1024 and 1025 vertices, either side of the device's
own cap, including ones whose vertices are all identical;
a per-vertex lighting array
.I shorter
than the vertex array;
and matrices that scale by zero or by
.if n 1e30
.if t 10\u\s-230\s+2\d
before geometry is pushed through them.
.PP
After each family the test draws a known colour into a small image and
reads it back with
.IR readpixels .
That answers the only question being asked \- is the draw device still
serving this process \- and it depends on nothing outside the process,
so it needs neither a screenshot nor a window manager to be believed.
A fault does not reach the report at all: the last line simply never
arrives.
.PP
The run has a deadline, and passing it prints which family was in
progress before killing the process group, because a hang reports
nothing at all and the harness that ran it can only say the run stopped.
This is not decoration.
Against the device provider the whole battery takes about five seconds;
against the Limbo
.B /dis/math/draw3d.dis
it does not finish, because that resampler walks the sprite's own box
and only then asks whether each pixel lands in the destination, where
the C path clips the box to the destination first and so does bounded
work however large the width.
A width of 100000 is already enough to stop it.
.PP
The last line is
.B PASS
or
.BR FAIL .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/draw3dsafetest.dis
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/draw3dsafetest.dis /dis/math/draw3d.dis
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/draw3dsafetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR draw (3),
.IR sprite2dtest (1),
.IR draw3dbench (1)
.SH BUGS
It needs a window, so it runs under
.I emu\-cocoa
and not
.IR emu\-g .
.PP
It reaches the protocol only through the
.IR draw3d (2)
module, so a message the Limbo encoder cannot produce is not covered:
a truncated one, a bad matrix selector, or a vertex count that disagrees
with the bytes that follow it.
Those are exactly the cases the C parser's own length checks exist for,
and nothing here tests those checks.
.PP
No case in this file has ever been observed to fault a released
.IR emu .
It was written against a deliberately broken one \- a mask coordinate
pushed off the end of its image by hand, which segfaults the parser \-
and it catches that.
Whether any argument reachable from Limbo can do the same on its own is
not known, and the evidence so far says not: with the bounds check in
.I d3maskset
instrumented to report, neither this test nor
.IR sprite2dtest (1)
ever reached it.
.PP
A test whose subject is that nothing happens grows stale quietly.
The value here is in the negative control, so anyone changing the C
sprite or polygon loops should break one on purpose and confirm this
still notices.
